exports[`release-notes.md > matches snapshot 1`] = `
"# Example test suite

This is an example test suite to demonstrate the features of the autograder framework.

It includes various types of tests and hooks to show how they can be used in practice.

If you are currently looking at the final \`release-notes.md\` file, you can find the
code for this test suite in \`tests/example.spec.ts\` file.

----

Score: **260 / 360**

Passed: **7 / 8**

----

## Summary

| Ok? | Test name | Status | Points |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| ✅ | [Simple Hello World](#simple_hello_world) | passed | 10 / 10 |
| ✅ | [Bash Hello World](#bash_hello_world) | passed | 20 / 20 |
| ✅ | [Running Node.js scripts](#running_nodejs_scripts) | passed | 30 / 30 |
| ✅ | [Compiling and running Java code](#compiling_and_running_java_code) | passed | 40 / 40 |
| ✅ | [Running Python scripts](#running_python_scripts) | passed | 50 / 50 |
| ❌ | [Failing test case example](#failing_test_case_example) | failed | 0 / 60 |
| ✅ | [Running Docker containers](#running_docker_containers) | passed | 70 / 70 |
| ⚠️ | [Custom grader function](#custom_grader_function) | passed | 40 / 80 |

## Test cases

<a name="simple_hello_world"><!-- anchor for linking from the table of contents --></a>

### Simple Hello World (10 / 10 points) [✅ passed]

Runs the hello world script and passes if there are no errors. This test doesn't check the output at all,
it just demonstrates how to run a command and assign points based on whether it succeeded or not.

This test does not have an explicit score, so it will use the default score specified in the suite.

<code>HTML</code> and **Markdown** formatting is supported in the descriptions for test cases, although you should not abuse this feature in real life.

\`\`\`
$ echo 'Hello world!'

Hello world!
\`\`\`

<a name="bash_hello_world"><!-- anchor for linking from the table of contents --></a>

### Bash Hello World (20 / 20 points) [✅ passed]

Runs two commands and checks that two separate strings are present in the output.

\`\`\`
$ echo 'Hello world!' > hello.tmp

[ no output ]
\`\`\`

\`\`\`
$ cat hello.tmp

Hello world!
\`\`\`

<a name="running_nodejs_scripts"><!-- anchor for linking from the table of contents --></a>

### Running Node.js scripts (30 / 30 points) [✅ passed]

Runs a JavaScript file and checks its output.

\`\`\`
$ node demo/hello.js

Hello from JavaScript!
\`\`\`

<a name="compiling_and_running_java_code"><!-- anchor for linking from the table of contents --></a>

### Compiling and running Java code (40 / 40 points) [✅ passed]

In this case, we compile a Java file in \`$setup\` before running it in \`$run\`.

\`\`\`
$ javac demo/Hello.java

[ no output ]
\`\`\`

\`\`\`
$ java -cp demo Hello

Hello from Java!
\`\`\`

<a name="running_python_scripts"><!-- anchor for linking from the table of contents --></a>

### Running Python scripts (50 / 50 points) [✅ passed]

Runs a Python file and checks its output. Also outputs the Python and pip versions for debugging purposes.

\`\`\`
$ python3 --version
$ python3 -m pip --version

Python 3.14.2
pip 26.1.1 from /home/codespace/.python/current/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip (python 3.14)
\`\`\`

\`\`\`
$ python3 demo/hello.py

Hello from Python!
\`\`\`

<a name="failing_test_case_example"><!-- anchor for linking from the table of contents --></a>

### Failing test case example (0 / 60 points) [❌ failed]

This test is designed to fail to demonstrate how failed tests are reported.

\`\`\`
$ ./this/command/does/not/exist.sh

bash: line 1: ./this/command/does/not/exist.sh: No such file or directory
\`\`\`

\`\`\`
❌ Error: bash: line 1: ./this/command/does/not/exist.sh: No such file or directory
\`\`\`

<a name="running_docker_containers"><!-- anchor for linking from the table of contents --></a>

### Running Docker containers (70 / 70 points) [✅ passed]

Docker images can be built and run within the autograder. Tests with Docker
commands may need a longer timeout since pulling and building images can take some time.

Note that for this test to work, Docker needs to be installed and running on the machine
where the tests are executed. Therefore we do not *actually* run the commands in this example.

\`\`\`
$ # you could build a Docker image for this test like this:
$ # docker build --file=demo/hello.Dockerfile --tag=hello demo
$ 
$ # for demonstration purposes, we just output the Dockerfile
$ cat demo/hello.Dockerfile

FROM hello-world

# you can do any setup you want here, like installing dependencies or copying files to be tested
\`\`\`

\`\`\`
$ # you can run the container like this:
$ # docker run --rm hello
$ 
$ # for demonstration purposes, we just output the Dockerfile
$ cat demo/hello.Dockerfile

FROM hello-world

# you can do any setup you want here, like installing dependencies or copying files to be tested
\`\`\`

<a name="custom_grader_function"><!-- anchor for linking from the table of contents --></a>

### Custom grader function (40 / 80 points) [⚠️ passed]

This test uses a custom grader function to determine the points awarded. It actually just gives half
the points and logs them in the test logs.

As you can see in the code, the \`customGrader\` function has access to the test context, including
the test case details and the logs. This allows for implementing complex grading logic based on various factors.

In a *real* autograder, you would inspect the logs and other context within the custom grader
to determine the points based on more complex logic than just whether the test passed or not.

\`\`\`
$ echo 'Random points for this example!'

Random points for this example!
\`\`\`

\`\`\`
$ Custom grader

Awarded 40 points based on custom grading logic.
\`\`\`"
`;

